== Marital Unity and Sexual Faithfulness

=== §. 1. #emph[Of the heads of those common-mutual duties.]

In the first part of this Treatise concerning #emph[man] and #emph[wife,] hath been declared, who are so to be accounted: in this second part their #emph[common-mutual duties] are to be laid forth. These are either #footnote[De necessitate ad esse.] #emph[absolutely necessary] for the #emph[being] and #emph[abiding] of marriage: or #footnote[De honestate ad benè esse.] needful and requisite for the #emph[well being] and #emph[well abiding] of it, that is, for the good estate of marriage, and for a commendable, and comfortable living together.

There are two kinds of the former

+ #emph[Matrimonial Unity.]
+ #emph[Matrimonial Chastity.]

The latter also may be drawn to two heads: for they are either such as the married couple are #emph[mutually] to perform #emph[each to other:] or such as both of them are #emph[jointly] to perform to #emph[others.]

Those mutual duties are

+ #emph[A loving affection of one to another.]
+ #emph[A provident care of one for another.]

Under that #emph[provident care] I comprise both the #emph[Means] whereby it may be the better effected (which is #emph[Cohabitation)] and the #emph[Matter] wherein it consisteth:

And this respecteth

+ The #emph[Soul]
+ The #emph[Body]
+ The #emph[Good-name]
+ The #emph[Goods]

of each other.

The #emph[joint] duties which are to be performed #emph[to others,]

respect

+ Those who are #emph[in the house.]
+ Those who are #emph[out of the house.]

They who are in the house are

+ #emph[Members] of the family.
+ #emph[Guests] which come to the family.

Many more particulars are comprised under these general heads, which I purpose distinctly to deliver, as I come to them in their several proper places.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of Matrimonial unity.]

The first, highest, chiefest, and most absolutely necessary common-mutual duty betwixt man and wife, is #emph[Matrimonial Unity,] whereby husband and wife do account one another to be #emph[one flesh,] and accordingly preserve the inviolable union whereby they are knit together. This is that duty which the Apostle enjoineth to husbands and wives, in these words, #emph[Let] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 10, 11.] #emph[not the wife depart from her husband: Let not the husband put away his wife.] He there speaketh of renouncing each other, and making the matrimonial bond frustrate, and of no effect: which bond he would have to be kept firm and inviolable, and they #emph[two] who are thereby made #emph[one,] constantly to remain #emph[one,] and not to make themselves #emph[two] again. This #emph[matrimonial unity] is so necessary, as it may not be dis-united or dissolved though one be a Christian, the other a Pagan. #emph[If any brother] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 12, 13.] (saith the Apostle) #emph[hath a wife that believeth not, let him not put her away. And the woman which hath an husband that believeth not, let her not leave him.]

The reasons of this inviolable union are especially two: One taken from the #emph[Author] of marriage: the other from the #emph[nature] thereof.

1. The #emph[Author] of marriage is God. It is his ordinance: and #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 18, 22, 24] he it is that by his ordinance hath made of #emph[two, one flesh.] Now mark the consequence which Christ as a ruled case, and undeniable principle inferreth thereon, #emph[What God hath joined together] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 6.] #emph[let no man put asunder:] If #emph[no man,] then nor wife, nor husband himself.

2. Such is the #emph[nature] of the matrimonial bond as it maketh of #emph[two one,] and #footnote[See Treat. 1 §. 79, 80.] more firmly bindeth them two together, then any other bond can bind any other two together, how then should they be #emph[two] again?

=== §. 3. #emph[Of Desertion.]

The vice contrary to matrimonial unity is #emph[Desertion,] when one of the married couple through indignation of the true religion, and utter detestation thereof, or some other like cause, shall apparently renounce all matrimonial unity, and withdraw him or her self from all society with the other, and live among Infidels, Idolaters, heretics, or other such persecutors, as a faithful Christian with safety of life, or a good conscience, cannot abide among; and though all good means that can be thought of be used to reclaim the party so departed, yet nothing will prevail, but obstinately persisteth in renouncing all matrimonial fellowship.

This #emph[Desertion] is in the case of marriage so capital, as it #footnote[#emph[Infidelis discedens in matrimonium peccare dignoscitur: itaque non est ei fides seruanda coniugij. Ambr. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7.] freeth the innocent party from any further seeking after the other. In which respect the Apostle saith, #emph[If the unbelieving depart, let him depart. A brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases,] 1 Cor. 7. 15. By bondage he meaneth matrimonial subjection (by reason whereof neither of the married persons have power of their own body, but one of the others.) Now they that are not under this bondage, are not bound to seek after it. That #emph[Desertion] therefore on the delinquents #footnote[τὴν λύσιν ἐκεῖνος ποιεῖ. #emph[Phot. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. #emph[Luth. de matrim. Calu. epist.] 227. #emph[Beza in lib. de Diuort.]] part is such a dissolution of marriage, as freeth the innocent party from the bondage thereof. In many reformed Churches beyond the seas #emph[Desertion] is accounted so far to dissolve the very bond of marriage, as liberty is given to the party forsaken to marry another: and it is also applied to other cases then that which is above mentioned: as when an #footnote[#emph[P. Martyr in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 13. #emph[Melanct. in loc. de Coniug.]] Infidel, Idolater, or Heretic shall depart from one of the true religion for other causes then hatred of religion: or when both man and wife having lived as Idolaters among Idolaters, #footnote[#emph[Zanchius de Oper. Dei lib.] 4. #emph[cap.] 1.] one of them being converted to the true faith, leaveth his abode among Idolaters, and goeth to the professors of the true faith, but can by no means get the other party to remove: or when one of the true religion shall depart from another of the same profession, and will by no means be brought to live with the party so left, but openly manifesteth peremptory obstinacy; the matter being heard and adjudged by the Magistrate, the marriage bond may be broken, and liberty given to the party forsaken to marry another. But because our Church hath no such custom, nor our law determined such cases, I leave them to the custom of other Churches.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of matrimonial Chastity.]

The second necessary common-mutual marriage-duty is #emph[Matrimonial chastity. Chastity] in a large extent is taken for all manner of purity in soul or body: in which respect the Apostle calleth the Church of God #emph[a chaste virgin.] But in the #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 11. 2.] sense wherein we here use it, it especially appertaineth to the body: which is that virtue whereby #emph[we possess our vessels] (to #footnote[1 #emph[Thess.] 4. 4.] use the Apostles phrase) #emph[in holiness and honour:] or more plainly to our purpose, whereby we keep our bodies undefiled.

Chastity thus restrained to the body is of

+ #emph[Single life.]
+ #emph[Wedlock.]

That of single life is opposed to fornication: and it is either of such as never were married. Such an one was #emph[S. Paul,] in which respect he wisheth that all were as he, 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 7. Or of such as are lawfully freed from the bond of marriage. Such an one the Apostle calleth #footnote[ὄντως χήρα. 1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 5. #emph[Paphnutius congressum cum legitima vxore castitatem esse dicebat. Socr. hist. Eccl. l.] 1. #emph[cap.] 11.] #emph[a widow indeed.] Chastity of wedlock is that virtue whereby parties married, observing the lawful and honest use of marriage, keep their bodies from being defiled with strange flesh: thus the Apostle commandeth #emph[wives] to be #emph[chaste, Tit.] 2. 5. So as #footnote[Qui leges coniugij seruat homini continenti par sit. Greg. Naz. Cygn. Carm.] they that keep the laws of wedlock are as chaste as they that contain.

Here by the way note the dotage of our adversaries, who think there is no chastity, but of single persons: whereupon in their speeches and writings #footnote[#emph[Clerici castitatem professi matrimonium contrahere non possunt. Concil. Trid.] §. 24. #emph[Can.] 9.] they oppose chastity and matrimony one to another, as two contraries.

Some of their holy fathers and Popes, and those not the least learned, nor of worst note among them, have inferred by their arguments against Priests marriage, that #emph[marriage is a living in the flesh, a sowing to the flesh, a pollution of the flesh.] To that #footnote[#emph[Siricius & Innocentius] 3. #emph[Gratian. Decr. distinct.] 31. 82. 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 5.] purpose #emph[S. Pauls] advice to man and wife to abstain, that they may give themselves to fasting and prayer, is urged: but directly contrary to the intent of the Apostle. For,

1. He speaketh there of extraordinary humiliation. 2. He interposeth this limitation, #emph[for a time.] 3. He saith not simply, #emph[that ye may pray,] but #emph[that ye may give your selves.] (or have #footnote[σχολάζητε. #emph[Non dixit simpliciter vt oretis, sed vt vacetis orationi: ac si rem impediat, non coinquinet. Chrys. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] leisure) #emph[to prayer:] as if it did only hinder, but not pollute prayer.

But how can the forenamed spots and blots of marriage stand with that beauty and glory wherewith the Apostle setteth it forth in these words, #emph[marriage is honourable in all, Heb.] 13. 4. If marriage were as Papists set it forth to be, the #emph[marriage-bed] were very unfitly called #emph[a bed undefiled.]

Behold how contrary the spirits of #emph[S. Paul] and of their Popes were. I wot well far more contrary then chastity and matrimony.

But to return to our matter, clear it is, that married persons may be chaste, and accordingly they ought to be chaste. To which purpose the Apostle counselling men and women, for avoiding fornication, to have wives and husbands, inserteth this particle OWN #emph[(Let every man have his] OWN #emph[wife,] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2. ἑαυτου. ἴδιον.] #emph[and every woman have her] OWN #emph[husband)] whereby he implieth, that they should not have to do with any other. That which #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 5. 19. See more of this text in Treat. 4. §. 11.] #emph[Solomon] expresseth of an husband, by the rule of relation must be applied to a wife. As the man must be satisfied at all times in his wife, and even ravished with her love; so must the woman be satisfied at all times in her husband, and even ravished with his love. By the like rule the precept given to wives, #emph[to be chaste,] must husbands take as directed to themselves #footnote[#emph[Tit.] 2. 5.] also, and be #emph[chaste.] This duty did #emph[Isaac] and #emph[Rebekah] faithfully and mutually perform each to other.

1. It was one main end, why marriage (especially since the #footnote[Reasons for marriage-chastity.] fall of man) was ordained, to live #emph[chastely.] This the Apostle implieth, where he saith, #emph[to avoid fornication,] let every man have his own wife, and let every woman have her own husband. #footnote[1\. Chastity one end of marriage. 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2. #emph[Ibid. vers.] 9.] And again, #emph[If they cannot contain let them marry.]

2. By chastity is a godly seed preserved on earth. By this reason doth the Prophet #emph[Malachy] enforce this duty. For after he had said, that the Lord #emph[made one,] meaning of two one flesh #footnote[2\. By chastity a godly seed preserved. #emph[Mal.] 2. 16.] by marriage, he inferreth this exhortation, #emph[Therefore take heed to your spirit, and let none deal unfaithfully against the wife of his youth.]

3. An especial part of the honour of marriage consisteth in #footnote[3\. Chastity the honour of marriage. #emph[Heb.] 13. 4.] chastity: whereupon the Apostle having given this high commendation of marriage, that it is #emph[honourable in all,] addeth this clause #emph[(and the bed undefiled)] to show the reason of that honour. As if he had said, Because the marriage-bed is in it self a bed undefiled, marriage is therefore in it self honourable, and doth so far remain honourable, as the bed remaineth undefiled.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of Adultery.]

The vice contrary to matrimonial chastity is #emph[Adultery,] one of the most capital vices in that estate: a vice whereby way is made for #emph[Divorce:] as is clear and evident by the determination of Christ himself, concerning that point, first #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 5. 32.] propounded in his sermon on the mount, and again #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 19. 9. #emph[Christus ab adultera vero separari permittit. Greg. Naz. Orat.] 36. #emph[Vir post fornicationem non est vir, Chrys. hom.] 19. #emph[in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7.] repeated in his conference with the Pharisees, where condemning unjust divorces, he excepteth the divorce made for adultery.

And great reason there is thereof. For #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 16.] the adulterer maketh himself one flesh with his harlot. Why then should he remain to be one flesh with his wife? #emph[Two] (saith the Law) #emph[shall be one flesh:] not three. The like may be said of a wife committing adultery.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of pardoning adultery upon repentance.]

#emph[Quest.] Seeing by adultery just cause of divorce is given, may this fault upon the repentance of the delinquent person be so forgiven, as no divorce be sought by the innocent person, but both continue to live together in wedlock as before?

#emph[Answ.] Though it be not meet in this case, to impose it as an inviolable law upon the innocent party, to retain the delinquent, because of repentance (for we have no direct and strict warrant for it) yet I doubt not but they may so do, if they will, and that without just exception to the contrary, they ought so to do. For the law of divorce did not necessarily enjoin any to sue out the bill, but only afforded them liberty to use that punishment if they saw cause. I doubt not but for warrant of this liberty, we may take Gods pattern, in retaining Churches and people after they have committed spiritual adultery: and Christs forgiving the woman that had committed adultery. For, #footnote[#emph[Postquam Christus ait adulterae, nec ego te damnabo: vade, deinceps noli peccare, quis non intelligat, debere maritum ignoscere quod videt ignouisse Dominum amborum: nec iam se debere adulteram dicere cuius poenitentis crimen diuina credit miseratione deletam? Aug. de adult. Coniug. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 6.] #emph[Seeing Christ said to an adulteress, I condemn thee not, go and sin no more, who cannot conceive that an husband ought to] #emph[forgive that which he seeth the Lord both of husband and wife hath forgiven: and that he ought not to account her an adulteress, whose fault he believeth to be blotted out, by the mercy of God, upon her repentance?]

=== §. 7. #emph[Of the difference of adultery in a man, and in a wife.]

#emph[Quest.] Is the bond of marriage as much violated on the #footnote[Adultery as great a sin in a man as in his wife. #emph[Lex Jul. de Adult. l.] 1. #emph[Orif. Codic. in l. Illust. Greg. Sayr. Clau. reg. l.] 9. #emph[c.] 16.] mans part when he committeth adultery as on the womans when she doth so?

#emph[Answ.] Though the ancient Romans and Canonists have aggravated the womans fault in this kind far above the mans, and given the man more privileges then the woman, yet I see not how that difference in the sin can stand with the tenor of Gods word. I deny not but that more inconveniences may follow upon the womans default then upon the mans: as, greater infamy before men, worse disturbance #footnote[More inconveniences of a womans adultery, then a mans.] of the family, more mistaking of legitimate, or illegitimate children, with the like. The man cannot so well know which be his own children, as the woman; he may take base children to be his own, and so cast the inheritance upon them; and suspect his own to be basely born, and so deprive them of their patrimony. But the woman is freed from all such mistakings. Yet in regard of the breach of wedlock, and transgression against God, the sin of either party is alike. Gods word maketh no disparity betwixt them. At the beginning God said of them both, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 5.] #emph[they two shall be one flesh:] not the woman only with the man, but the man also with the woman is made #emph[one flesh.] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 3, 4.] Their power also over one another in this respect is alike. If on just occasion they abstain, #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 5.] it must be with mutual consent. If the husband leave his wife, #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 15.] she is as free, as he should be, if she left him. Accordingly the punishment #footnote[#emph[Tanto grauius viros puniri oportet, quanto magis ad eos pertinet & virtute vincere, & exemplo regere foeminas. Aug. de adult. Coniug. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 8.] which by Gods law was to be inflicted on Adulterers is the same, whether the man or the woman be the delinquent, #emph[(Deut.] 22. 22.) If difference be made, it is meet that adulterous husbands be so much the more severely punished, by how much the more it appertaineth to them to excel in virtue, and to govern their wives by example.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of the heinousness of Adultery.]

But to return to the discovery of the heinousness of Adultery, I find no sin throughout the whole Scripture so notoriously in the several colours thereof set forth, as it is. For besides that it is #footnote[7\. Commandment.] by name forbidden in the Decalogue, it is further expressly branded to be committed,

1. Against #emph[each person in the holy Trinity:] the #emph[father] (whose #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 2. 17.] covenant is broken) the #emph[son] (whose #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 16.] members are made the members of an harlot) and the #emph[Holy Ghost] (whose #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 19.] Temple is polluted.)

2. Against ones #emph[neighbour,] as #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 34. 31.] the party with whom the sin is committed (for this sin cannot be committed singly by one alone) the #emph[husband] and #emph[wife] of each party #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 6. 35.] (who cannot rest contented with any satisfaction) the #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 21. 10. #emph[Judg.] 11. 2. #emph[Deut.] 23. 2.] #emph[children] born in adultery (whom they brand with an indelible character of infamy, and deprive of many privileges that otherwise they might enjoy) the #footnote[#emph[Levit.] 21. 9. #emph[Gen.] 34. 7. 2 #emph[Sam.] 13. 22.] #emph[alliance] and #emph[friends] of each party (to whom the grief and disgrace of this foul sin reacheth) the #emph[whole family] appertaining to either of them (for #footnote[#emph[Job] 31. 12. 2 #emph[Sam.] 12. 10.] this is as a fire in an house) the #emph[town, city,] and #emph[nation] where such unclean birds roost (for #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 34. 27. #emph[Judg.] 20. 10. #emph[Hos.] 4. 3.] all they lie open to the vengeance of God for this sin) and the very #emph[Church of God] (the #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 15.] holy seed whereof is by this sin hindered).

3. Against the #emph[parties] themselves that commit this sin, and that against their #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 6. 32.] souls, #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 18.] bodies, #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 6. 33.] name, #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 6. 26.] goods, and all that appertaineth to them.

As this sin is in it self a sinful sin, so by the bitter and #footnote[The bitter fruits of Adultery.] cursed fruits which proceed from it, it is made out of measure sinful. For

1. By it husbands and wives affection (which of all other ought to be the most inviolable) is so alienated, as seldom it is reconciled again.

2. By it the goods of the family are much wasted: the adulterous husband spending that wherewith he should provide for his family, on his harlot: and the adulterous wife purloining what she can from her husband.

3. By it husbands and wives are stirred up to wish, and long after one anothers death: and not only inwardly in heart to wish it, but outwardly also in deed to practise it.

4. If from this sin there arise not a galling and terrifying conscience (as oft there doth) then (which is worse) a seared conscience, an hard heart, a reprobate sense, and an impudent face.

Wherefore God accordingly deals with such sinners. In #footnote[Gods judgments against adultery.] his soul #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 22. 14.] he #emph[hateth them:] by his word he hath denounced many fearful judgments against them, both in #footnote[#emph[Hos.] 4. 2, 3.] this world, and in #footnote[#emph[Rev.] 21. 8.] the world to come (against no sin more.) This sin is reckoned to be one of the most principal causes of the greatest judgments that ever were inflicted in the world: as of the #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 6. 2, #emph[etc.]] general deluge: of that #footnote[#emph[Ezek.] 16. 50.] fire and brimstone which destroyed #emph[Sodom] and #emph[Gomorrah:] of #footnote[#emph[Levit.] 18. 28.] Canaans spewing out her Inhabitants: of that #footnote[#emph[Num.] 25. 9.] plague which destroyed in one day 24000: and of the #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 5. 7, 8, 9.] Israelites captivity, with the like: By all which we see that fearful doom verified, #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 13. 4.] #emph[Whoremongers, and adulterers God will judge.] Now consider what #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 10. 31.] #emph[a fearful thing it is to fall into the hands of the living God.]

=== §. 9. #emph[Of remedies against Adultery, and in particular of] due benevolence, #emph[and of defect or excess therein.]

For preventing this heinous sin (to omit many other remedies which Gods word hath prescribed, as #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 6. 25.] a diligent keeping of the #emph[heart] (that lustful thoughts proceed not from thence) of the #footnote[#emph[Job] 31. 1.] #emph[eyes] (that they wander not on the beauty or properness of any ones person, or on lascivious pictures, or any other like allurements) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 7. 21.] #emph[ears] (that they hearken not to any enticements of others) of the #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 5. 4.] #emph[tongue] (that it utter no unchaste and corrupt communication) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 7. 13.] #emph[lips] (that they delight not in wanton kisses) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 6. 29.] #emph[hands] (that they use no wanton dalliance) of the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 7. 25.] #emph[feet] (that they carry thee not too near to the place where adultery may be committed) of thy #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 5. 7.] #emph[company] (that thou be not defiled with others wantonness and uncleanness) of thy #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 19. 33.] #emph[diet] (that it be not immoderate) of thine #footnote[#emph[Ezek.] 23. 6, 15, 40.] #emph[apparel] (that it be not garish and lascivious) of thy #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 39. 9. #emph[Pro.] 2. 16.] #emph[time] (that it be not vainly and idly spent) to omit, I say, these and other like remedies.) One of the best remedies that can be prescribed to married persons (next to an #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 39. 9. #emph[Pro.] 2. 16.] awful fear of God, and a continual setting of him before them, wheresoever they are) is, that husband and wife mutually delight each in other, and maintain a pure and fervent love betwixt themselves, yielding that #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2, 3, 5, 9.] #emph[due benevolence] one to another which is warranted & sanctified by Gods word, and ordained of God for this particular end. This #emph[due benevolence] (as the Apostle styleth it) is one of the most proper and essential acts of marriage: and necessary for the main and principal ends thereof: as for preservation of chastity in such as have not the gift of continency, for increasing the world with a legitimate brood, and for linking the affections of the married couple more firmly together. These ends of marriage, at least the two former, are made void without this duty be performed.

As it is called #footnote[εὔνοια.] #emph[benevolence] because it must be performed with good will and delight, willingly, readily and cheerfully; so it is said to be #footnote[ὀφειλομένη.] #emph[due] because it is a debt which the wife oweth to her husband, and he to her. #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 4.] #emph[For the wife hath not the power of her own body, but the husband; and likewise also the husband hath not the power of his own body, but the wife.]

I have my warrant from the Apostle to prescribe this duty as a remedy against adultery. For #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2, 3.] #emph[to avoid fornication,] he adviseth man and wife to #emph[render due benevolence] one to another. If then this question be moved #emph[(How will marriage keep men and women from adultery?)] this answer out of the Apostles words may be given #emph[(by rendering due benevolence:)] which he further inculcateth by declaring the mischief that may follow upon the neglect of this duty, namely #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 5.] #emph[a casting of themselves into the snares of Satan.] Well might he press this duty to that end, because no other means is of like force: nor fasting, nor watching, nor hard lodging, nor long travel, nor much labour, nor cold, nor solitariness, nor any thing else. #footnote[Dum solitudinis me deserta vallarent, ardorem naturae ferre non poteram: quem cùm crebris ieiunijs frangerem, mens cogitationibus aestuabat. Hier. ad Rust. Mon. Lege eundem ad Eustoc. de custod. virgin.] Some that have by these means endeavoured much to beat down their bodies, and subdue lust (but neglected the forenamed remedy) have notwithstanding felt lust boiling in them.

There are two extremes contrary to this duty. One in the #emph[defect:] another in the #emph[excess.]

#emph[Defect] therein is, when in case of need it is not required, or being required by the one, it is not yielded by the other. Modesty is pretended by some for not requiring it: but in a duty so warrantable and needful, pretence of modesty is (to speak the least) a sign of great infirmity, and a cause of much iniquity. To deny this duty being justly required, is to deny a due debt, and to give Satan great advantage. The punishment inflicted on #emph[Onan, (Gen.] 38. 9, 10.) showeth how great a wrong this is. From that punishment #footnote[#emph[Hebraei inquiunt perinde vt homicidam reum esse qui temerè semen profundit. Merc. in Gen.] 38. 9.] the Hebrews gather that this sin is a kind of murder. It is so much the more heinous when hatred, stoutness, niceness, fear of having too many children, or any other like respects, are the cause thereof.

#emph[Excess] is either in the #emph[measure,] or in the #emph[time. In the measure,] when husband or wife is insatiable; provoking, rather then assuaging lust, and weakening their natural vigor more then suppressing their unnatural humor. Many husbands and wives are much oppressed by their bedfellows unsatiableness in this kind.

#emph[In the time,] when it is against #emph[Piety, Mercy,] or #emph[Modesty.]

1. #emph[Against Piety,] when no day, nor duty of Religion, no not extraordinary days, and duties of humiliation, will make them forbear. #footnote[#emph[Joel] 2. 16.] The Prophets bidding #emph[the bridegroom and bride go out of their chamber in the day of a Fast,] and #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 5.] the Apostles #emph[excepting of Prayer and fasting,] where he enjoineth this duty of due benevolence, show that in the time of a Fast it must be forborne.

2. #emph[Against Mercy,] when one of the married couple being weak by sickness, pain, labour, travel, or any other like means, and through that weakness not well able to perform his duty, the other notwithstanding will have it performed. #emph[I will have mercy, and not sacrifice,] saith the Lord. Shall Gods sacrifice give place to mercy, and shall not mans or womans lust? For so I may well term this unseasonable desire.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What if an husband or wife continue so long sick, or otherwise weak, as the other cannot contain?]

#emph[Answ.] In such cases of necessity the body must be beaten down, and earnest prayer made for the gift of continency: for assuredly the Lord who hath brought thee to that necessity, will give thee grace sufficient.

3. #emph[Against Modesty,] #footnote[#emph[Quo tempore si vir coierit cum muliere, dicuntur concepti foetus vitium seminis trahere: ita vt leprosi & elephantiaci ex hac conceptione nascantur. Hier. in Lam.] 1.] when husbands require this duty in that time, which under the Law was called #emph[the time of a wives separation for her disease (Lev.] 15. 19. etc.) For what can be expected from such polluted copulation, but a leprous and loathsome generation? This kind of intemperancy is expressly forbidden #emph[(Levit.] 18. 19.) and a capital punishment inflicted on such as offended therein #emph[(Levit.] 20. 18.) Abstinence in this time is set in the catalogue of those notes which declare a man to be righteous #emph[(Ezek.] 18. 6.) and the contrary intemperancy is put in the roll of such abominations as provoked God to spew out the Canaanites #emph[(Levit.] 18. 28.) and to forsake his own inheritance #emph[(Ezek.] 22. 10.)

To this kind of intemperancy #footnote[#emph[Aug. de bon. coniug. ca.] 6. #emph[Amb. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7.] some refer a mans knowing of his wife after she hath conceived with child. But I find no such matter condemned in Gods word: neither dare I make that a sin which is not there condemned. #footnote[#emph[Nemo Essenorum cum praegnante concumbit. Joseph. de bel. Jud. li.] 2. #emph[c.] 7.] Certain Sectaries among the Jews are branded for this error.

#strong[1. #emph[Object.] No other creature will so do: so as it may seem to be against nature.]

#emph[Answ.] 1. I deny the argument: though some forbear, yet all do not.

2. I deny the consequence: for other creatures are not so tied one male to one female, as an husband to his own wife. #footnote[#emph[Quod in bestijs ipso naturae iure praescriptum est, vt praegnantes ad partum vsque non coeant, hoc in hominibus sciant arbitrio relictum. Hier. in Eph.] 5. #emph[Sanchez de Matr. lib.] 9. #emph[disp.] 21, 22. #emph[agit de petendo & reddendo debito quando menstruum est: & tempore praegnationis, & dum infans lactatur.]] Besides, that which beasts by nature are tied unto, must be left to mans discretion.

#strong[2. #emph[Object.] After a woman hath once conceived, no more conceptions can be expected, till she be delivered.]

#emph[Answ.] Conception is not the only end of this duty: for it is to be rendered to such as are barren.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What if the wife give suck to her child, ought not her husband then to forbear?]

#emph[Answ.] Because giving suck is a mother duty, man ought to do what he can to contain.
